.TH "fi_xpu" "3" "2026\-11\-21" "Libfabric Programmer\(cqs Manual" "#VERSION#"
.SH NAME
fi_xpu \- OFI XPU API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<rdma/fi_xpu.h>\f[R]
.EE
.SH OVERVIEW
The FI_XPU capability and the interfaces described in this man page
should be considered experimental.
Until the experimental tag is removed, the interfaces, semantics, and
data structures associated with FI_XPU may change between library
versions.
.PP
The FI_XPU capability allows for a specified XPU (or device) to access a
given libfabric allocated resource for data path operations, with
control operations being managed by host (CPU) software.
A resource (endpoint, completion queue, counter) which has been
configured with this capability may only be accessed by the given XPU
for any data path operation, such as submitting data transfers or
reading completions.
.PP
As a general rule, control path operations may only be invoked by the
host CPU, while data path functions may only be accessed by a specified
XPU.
The following objects may be exported to an XPU: EP, CQ, and counters.
AV and MR lookups produce raw data usable by the XPU but the objects
themselves remain host\-only.
Detailed XPU behavior is documented in the corresponding man pages
(\f[CR]fi_av\f[R](3), \f[CR]fi_endpoint\f[R](3), \f[CR]fi_cq\f[R](3),
\f[CR]fi_cntr\f[R](3), \f[CR]fi_mr\f[R](3)).
.SH XPU CONTEXT
Related EP, CQ, and counter resources created with FI_XPU must all
target the same XPU.
The \f[CR]fid_xpu_ctx\f[R] object groups these resources together and
binds them to a specific device.
An EP created with FI_XPU may also be bound to a host (non\-XPU) CQ for
error handling.
It also provides the avenue for querying provider\-specific sizes needed
to interact with AV and MR data from the XPU.
.PP
An XPU context is created from a domain:
.IP
.EX
int fi_xpu_ctx(\f[B]struct\f[R] fid_domain *domain, \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_xpu_attr *attr,
               \f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu_ctx **ctx, void *context);
.EE
.PP
The number of XPU contexts that a domain supports is indicated by the
\f[CR]max_xpu_ctx_cnt\f[R] field in \f[CR]fi_domain_attr\f[R].
A value of 0 indicates no support for XPU\-initiated communication.
A value of 1 indicates a 1:1 mapping between a domain and an XPU.
.PP
The XPU context is passed to EP, CQ, and counter creation calls via a
pointer in their respective attribute structures
(\f[CR]fi_ep_attr\->xpu_ctx\f[R], \f[CR]fi_cq_attr\->xpu_ctx\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_cntr_attr\->xpu_ctx\f[R]).
XPU resources bound to the same EP must share the same XPU context.
A host CQ (without xpu_ctx set) may also be bound to an XPU EP for error
handling.
.PP
The XPU context is also passed to \f[CR]fi_av_lookup2\f[R] and
\f[CR]fi_mr_get_xpu_desc\f[R] so that the provider can return data in
the appropriate format for the target XPU.
This allows AV and MR objects to be shared across multiple XPUs \(em the
same AV or MR can be queried with different XPU contexts to get
device\-specific representations.
.SH fi_xpu_attr
The \f[CR]fi_xpu_attr\f[R] structure is an input argument that
identifies the target XPU device and provides optional memory management
callbacks.
It is passed to \f[CR]fi_xpu_ctx()\f[R] during context creation.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_xpu_ops {
    size_t size;
    int (*alloc)(uint64_t device, uint64_t size,
                 uint64_t alignment, uint64_t flags,
                 void **addr, int *fd, uint64_t *offset);
    int (*import)(uint64_t device, void *host_addr,
                  uint64_t size, int fd, uint64_t flags,
                  void **dev_addr);
    int (*unimport)(uint64_t device, void *host_addr);
    void (*free)(uint64_t device, void *addr);
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_xpu_attr {
    int                  iface;    \f[I]/* enum fi_hmem_iface */\f[R]
    uint64_t             device;
    \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_xpu_ops    *ops;
};
.EE
.TP
\f[I]iface\f[R]
The heterogeneous memory interface type (e.g., FI_HMEM_CUDA,
FI_HMEM_ZE).
.TP
\f[I]device\f[R]
The XPU device ordinal.
.TP
\f[I]ops\f[R]
Optional pointer to a \f[CR]fi_xpu_ops\f[R] structure containing memory
management callbacks.
If NULL, the provider uses its default mechanisms.
.SS fi_xpu_ops
The \f[CR]fi_xpu_ops\f[R] structure groups memory management callbacks
for XPU device memory.
The provider calls these when it needs to allocate, import, unimport or
free device memory on behalf of the XPU context.
.TP
\f[I]size\f[R]
Must be set to \f[CR]sizeof(struct fi_xpu_ops)\f[R].
This allows future expansion of the structure while maintaining backward
compatibility.
The provider uses this field to determine which callback fields are
present.
.TP
\f[I]alloc\f[R]
Allocate XPU memory.
The provider calls this when it needs device memory (e.g., for hardware
queue buffers).
If the \f[CR]FI_XPU_ALLOC_DMABUF\f[R] flag is set, the consumer must
also export a DMA\-BUF file descriptor so the provider can DMA directly
to the memory.
.TP
\f[I]import\f[R]
Map a host virtual address into the XPU address space.
The provider calls this when it has a host\-side address (BAR MMIO or
host RAM) that the XPU kernel needs to access.
If the \f[CR]FI_XPU_IMPORT_DMABUF\f[R] flag is set, the provider must
also pass a DMA\-BUF file descriptor that the consumer can use to set up
the mapping.
This is used for consumers that can only import certain address types
(e.g.\ MMIO) as a DMA\-BUF object.
Other flags indicate the memory type: \f[CR]FI_XPU_IMPORT_IOMEMORY\f[R]
for PCIe BAR MMIO regions, \f[CR]FI_XPU_IMPORT_DEVICEMAP\f[R] for
addresses that must be accessible from XPU kernels.
.TP
\f[I]unimport\f[R]
Release a mapping made by import.
The provider calls this when the object that needed the mapping is
closed, passing the same host address it passed to import.
.TP
\f[I]free\f[R]
Release memory previously allocated via alloc.
.SS Memory Callback Flags
.TP
\f[I]FI_XPU_ALLOC_DMABUF\f[R]
Passed to the alloc callback.
Indicates the allocation must be exportable as a DMA\-BUF fd for
provider access.
.TP
\f[I]FI_XPU_IMPORT_IOMEMORY\f[R]
Passed to the import callback.
Indicates the host address points to PCIe BAR MMIO (device I/O memory).
.TP
\f[I]FI_XPU_IMPORT_DEVICEMAP\f[R]
Passed to the import callback.
Indicates the resulting pointer must be accessible from XPU kernel code.
.TP
\f[I]FI_XPU_IMPORT_DMABUF\f[R]
Passed to the import callback.
Indicates the host address has been exported as a DMA\-BUF fd passed via
the \f[I]fd\f[R] parameter.
.SH fi_xpu_ctx_attr
The \f[CR]fi_xpu_ctx_attr\f[R] structure is returned by
\f[CR]fi_xpu_ctx_query()\f[R].
It contains provider\-specific output parameters for the given XPU
context.
.IP
.EX
#define FI_XPU_CAP_EP      (1ULL << 0)
#define FI_XPU_CAP_CQ      (1ULL << 1)
#define FI_XPU_CAP_CNTR    (1ULL << 2)
#define FI_XPU_CAP_WR      (1ULL << 3)

\f[B]struct\f[R] fi_xpu_ctx_attr {
    uint64_t     caps;
    size_t       av_addr_size;
    size_t       mr_desc_size;
    \f[I]/* Added in 2.8 */\f[R]
    size_t       max_tx_wr_desc_size;
    size_t       max_rx_wr_desc_size;
};
.EE
.TP
\f[I]caps\f[R]
Bitmask of XPU capabilities supported by this provider.
The application should check these flags before attempting to create XPU
resources.
Attempting to create an unsupported resource type will return
\-FI_ENOSYS.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]FI_XPU_CAP_EP\f[R]: Provider supports XPU endpoints.
This includes: device\-side data transfer operations (post/dispatch) for
the capabilities returned by \f[CR]fi_getinfo\f[R] (e.g., FI_MSG,
FI_TAGGED, FI_RMA, FI_ATOMIC), \f[CR]fi_ep_export_xpu\f[R] to export the
endpoint for device access, \f[CR]fi_av_lookup2\f[R] with FI_XPU to
retrieve raw AV addresses, and \f[CR]fi_mr_get_xpu_desc\f[R] with FI_XPU
to retrieve raw MR descriptors.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]FI_XPU_CAP_CQ\f[R]: Provider supports XPU completion queues.
This includes: \f[CR]fi_cq_export_xpu\f[R] to export the CQ for device
access, and the device\-side CQ functions (\f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_read\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_readfrom\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_readerr\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_sread\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_sreadfrom\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]FI_XPU_CAP_CNTR\f[R]: Provider supports XPU counters.
This includes: \f[CR]fi_cntr_export_xpu\f[R] to export the counter for
device access, and the device\-side counter functions
(\f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_read\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_readerr\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_wait\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_add\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_set\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_adderr\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_seterr\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]FI_XPU_CAP_WR\f[R]: Provider supports device\-side work requests.
This includes the device\-side work request functions
(\f[CR]fi_xpu_wr_prepare\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_wr_queue_tx\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_wr_queue_recv\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_wr_queue_trecv\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_tx_flush\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_recv_flush\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_xpu_trecv_flush\f[R], and the \f[CR]fi_xpu_wr_modify_*\f[R]
calls).
Requires \f[CR]FI_XPU_CAP_EP\f[R].
See \f[CR]fi_wr\f[R](3).
.TP
\f[I]av_addr_size\f[R]
Size in bytes of the raw address returned by \f[CR]fi_av_lookup2\f[R]
(when called with \f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] flag) for each AV entry.
All AV entries for a given context have the same size.
.TP
\f[I]mr_desc_size\f[R]
Size in bytes of the raw descriptor returned by
\f[CR]fi_mr_get_xpu_desc\f[R] (when called with \f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] flag).
All descriptors for a given context have the same size.
.TP
\f[I]max_tx_wr_desc_size\f[R] / \f[I]max_rx_wr_desc_size\f[R]
Size in bytes of the device memory a work request queued from the device
requires, for transmit and receive operations respectively, whether it
was prepared with \f[CR]fi_xpu_wr_prepare\f[R] or prepared on the host
and copied to the device.
Only valid when \f[I]caps\f[R] includes \f[CR]FI_XPU_CAP_WR\f[R].
These sizes may differ from \f[CR]fi_ep_attr::max_tx_wr_size\f[R] and
\f[CR]max_rx_wr_size\f[R], which apply to work requests queued on the
host.
Added in 2.8, so they are only filled in for applications requesting API
version 2.8 or later.
.SS fi_xpu_ctx_query
.IP
.EX
int fi_xpu_ctx_query(\f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu_ctx *ctx,
                     \f[B]struct\f[R] fi_xpu_ctx_attr *attr);
.EE
.PP
Query the provider for XPU context parameters.
.PP
Because \f[CR]fi_xpu_ctx_attr\f[R] may grow in future releases, the
provider selects the structure format and size to write based on the API
version the application requested.
Applications requesting version 2.7 receive the 2.7 format, containing
only \f[CR]caps\f[R], \f[CR]av_addr_size\f[R], and
\f[CR]mr_desc_size\f[R]; applications requesting 2.8 or later also
receive \f[CR]max_tx_wr_desc_size\f[R] and
\f[CR]max_rx_wr_desc_size\f[R].
.PP
The returned \f[CR]caps\f[R] field indicates which XPU objects the
provider supports (FI_XPU_CAP_EP, FI_XPU_CAP_CQ, FI_XPU_CAP_CNTR,
FI_XPU_CAP_WR).
The application should check these flags before attempting to create XPU
resources.
The returned \f[CR]av_addr_size\f[R] and \f[CR]mr_desc_size\f[R] fields
are used to allocate appropriately sized buffers for
\f[CR]fi_av_lookup2\f[R] and \f[CR]fi_mr_get_xpu_desc\f[R] calls.
Different XPU contexts (targeting different devices) may report
different sizes.
.SS EP
An XPU EP is created by calling \f[CR]fi_endpoint2\f[R] with
\f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] in the flags parameter and setting
\f[CR]fi_ep_attr\->xpu_ctx\f[R] to an open XPU context.
.PP
An EP created with FI_XPU may be bound to XPU CQs and XPU counters
created with the same XPU context.
Binding to a host (non\-XPU) CQ or counter is allowed but behavior is
provider\-specific.
Control functions such as \f[CR]fi_ep_bind\f[R], \f[CR]fi_enable\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_setopt\f[R], and CM operations are available only to the host
CPU.
.PP
Once bound and enabled, \f[CR]fi_ep_export_xpu\f[R] exports the EP for
device access.
The caller provides a \f[CR]struct fid_xpu_ep\f[R] which the provider
fills with the information needed for device\-side data transfer
functions.
.PP
Data transfer operations on the exported EP are only available to the
given XPU.
This includes \f[CR]fi_msg\f[R](3), \f[CR]fi_rma\f[R](3),
\f[CR]fi_tagged\f[R](3), \f[CR]fi_atomic\f[R](3).
See \f[CR]fi_endpoint\f[R](3) for details.
.SS CQ
An XPU CQ is created by setting \f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] in
\f[CR]fi_cq_attr\->flags\f[R] and \f[CR]fi_cq_attr\->xpu_ctx\f[R] to an
open XPU context before calling \f[CR]fi_cq_open\f[R].
Control operations (\f[CR]fi_cq_open\f[R], \f[CR]fi_close\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_control\f[R]) remain CPU\-only.
.PP
\f[CR]fi_cq_export_xpu\f[R] exports the CQ for device access.
The caller provides a \f[CR]struct fid_xpu_cq\f[R] which the provider
fills with the information needed for the device\-side completion
functions (\f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_read\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cq_readerr\f[R]).
Host\-side read operations (\f[CR]fi_cq_read\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_cq_sread\f[R]) are not available on an exported CQ.
See \f[CR]fi_cq\f[R](3) for details.
.SS Counters
An XPU counter is created by setting \f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] in
\f[CR]fi_cntr_attr\->flags\f[R] and \f[CR]fi_cntr_attr\->xpu_ctx\f[R] to
an open XPU context before calling \f[CR]fi_cntr_open\f[R].
Control operations (\f[CR]fi_cntr_open\f[R], \f[CR]fi_close\f[R]) remain
CPU\-only.
.PP
\f[CR]fi_cntr_export_xpu\f[R] exports the counter for device access.
The caller provides a \f[CR]struct fid_xpu_cntr\f[R] which the provider
fills with the information needed for the device\-side counter functions
(\f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_read\f[R], \f[CR]fi_xpu_cntr_wait\f[R]).
Host\-side read operations (\f[CR]fi_cntr_read\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_cntr_wait\f[R]) are not available on an exported counter.
See \f[CR]fi_cntr\f[R](3) for details.
.SS AV
The AV is not bound to an XPU context \(em it is a shared domain\-level
resource.
.PP
The application queries \f[CR]av_addr_size\f[R] from
\f[CR]fi_xpu_ctx_query()\f[R], allocates a buffer of that size, calls
\f[CR]fi_av_lookup2\f[R] with \f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] flag and the XPU context
for each entry to retrieve the raw address, and copies the results to
device\-accessible memory.
The raw address is a provider\-specific representation usable by the XPU
when posting work requests.
.PP
\f[CR]fi_av_lookup2\f[R] is an extended version of
\f[CR]fi_av_lookup\f[R] that accepts flags and an XPU context, allowing
the same AV to be queried for different XPU devices.
See \f[CR]fi_av\f[R](3) for details.
.SS MR
The MR is not bound to an XPU context \(em it is a shared domain\-level
resource.
The same MR can be queried with different XPU contexts.
.PP
The application queries \f[CR]mr_desc_size\f[R] from
\f[CR]fi_xpu_ctx_query()\f[R], allocates a buffer, calls
\f[CR]fi_mr_get_xpu_desc\f[R] with the \f[CR]FI_XPU\f[R] flag and the
XPU context to retrieve the raw descriptor (e.g., the hardware lkey),
and copies the result to device\-accessible memory.
The raw descriptor replaces the desc parameter in data transfer
operations initiated by the XPU.
.PP
\f[CR]fi_mr_get_xpu_desc\f[R] is an extended descriptor query that
accepts flags and an XPU context, allowing the same MR to be queried for
different XPU devices.
See \f[CR]fi_mr\f[R](3) for details.
.SH DEVICE\-SIDE API
The device\-side API provides communication functions callable from XPU
kernels.
Include the following header:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<rdma/fi_xpu_device.h>\f[R]
.EE
.PP
This header is compiled with a single XPU kernel compiler at a time.
It supports XPU programming environments that provide a C/C++ interface:
.IP \(bu 2
NVIDIA CUDA (nvcc)
.IP \(bu 2
AMD ROCm HIP (hipcc)
.IP \(bu 2
Intel oneAPI Level Zero / SYCL (icpx \-fsycl)
.PP
The same header covers all of the above \(em the \f[CR]FI_XPU_FUNC\f[R]
macro adapts the function qualifier (\f[CR]__device__\f[R],
\f[CR]static inline\f[R], etc.)
based on the compiler detected at build time.
.SS Provider Dispatch Model
The device\-side header uses a provider\-identifier based dispatch
model.
Each exported XPU handle (\f[CR]fid_xpu_ep\f[R], \f[CR]fid_xpu_cq\f[R],
\f[CR]fid_xpu_cntr\f[R]) embeds \f[CR]struct fid_xpu\f[R] as its first
member.
The provider populates \f[CR]fid_xpu.prov_id\f[R] during the export call
(\f[CR]fi_ep_export_xpu\f[R], \f[CR]fi_cq_export_xpu\f[R],
\f[CR]fi_cntr_export_xpu\f[R]) with its assigned value from
\f[CR]enum fi_xpu_provider\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]enum\f[R] fi_xpu_provider {
    FI_XPU_PROV_EFA = 1,
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu {
    uint32_t fclass;    \f[I]/* FI_CLASS_EP, _CQ, _CNTR */\f[R]
    uint32_t prov_id;   \f[I]/* enum fi_xpu_provider */\f[R]
    uint64_t prov_ctx;  \f[I]/* provider\-internal context */\f[R]
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu_ep {
    \f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu fid;
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu_cq {
    \f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu fid;
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu_cntr {
    \f[B]struct\f[R] fid_xpu fid;
};
.EE
.PP
The generic dispatch functions take the typed handle
(\f[CR]struct fid_xpu_ep *\f[R], \f[CR]fid_xpu_cq *\f[R], or
\f[CR]fid_xpu_cntr *\f[R]) and switch on \f[CR]fid.prov_id\f[R] to route
to the appropriate provider\-specific implementation.
The \f[CR]prov_ctx\f[R] field provides a mechanism for the provider to
locate all state associated with the resource \(em the provider stores
everything in device memory allocated via the XPU ops callbacks, and
\f[CR]prov_ctx\f[R] holds the address where that state resides.
.PP
The tight range of provider IDs (starting at 1) allows the compiler to
generate an efficient jump table rather than a chain of comparisons.
.SS Scope
The scope parameter specifies the set of threads that make the call
together.
This allows the implementation to optimize.
.PP
