.TE
.PP
FI_XPU_SUBGROUP, FI_XPU_WORK_GROUP and FI_XPU_DEVICE are referred to
below as group scopes.
.PP
At FI_XPU_WORK_ITEM a call is the calling thread\(cqs own, with
arguments of its own: the threads of a subgroup may each post to an
endpoint of their own, read a completion queue of their own or read a
counter of their own.
At a group scope every thread of the scope reaches the call and passes
identical arguments, and the call returns the same value in every
thread.
This holds alike for post, completion queue and counter calls.
A kernel may make the same call from several groups at the same time;
each group\(cqs call is its own.
.PP
A post call is a data transfer call (see Data Transfer Operations below)
or a work request call that queues or flushes operations (see Work
Requests).
It submits one operation for its scope, which behaves as one call of its
host counterpart does.
At FI_XPU_WORK_ITEM concurrent calls of different threads are separate
operations: each completes, and fails, as if it had been made alone.
At a group scope the arguments are those of the whole operation, and
memory written by the threads of the scope before the call is visible to
it, so that they can prepare a buffer together and transfer it with one
call.
Threads that each want an operation of their own, such as a write of
their own slice of a buffer, call at FI_XPU_WORK_ITEM with their own
arguments.
.PP
A post call returns 0 once its operation has been accepted, and
\-FI_EAGAIN when the provider currently lacks the resources to initiate
it, with the meaning \-FI_EAGAIN has for the host data transfer calls,
under the domain\(cqs resource management (see ERRORS in
\f[CR]fi_msg\f[R](3) and Resource Management in
\f[CR]fi_domain\f[R](3)): the operation may be retried once resources
become available, which may require the application to check for
completions.
.PP
When resources run out while several operations are requested at the
same time, the provider returns \-FI_EAGAIN for the ones it lacks the
resources for: if N operations are requested at once and the provider
has the resources for only M of them, where M is less than N, M of the
calls succeed and the other N \- M return \-FI_EAGAIN.
Which ones succeed is not defined, as with host threads that post to one
endpoint concurrently.
.PP
A kernel can handle \-FI_EAGAIN as a host thread does, by reading
completions between retries until the call succeeds.
Where the threads that post are not the ones that read completions, the
kernel has to let the posting threads know when resources have become
available.
One way to avoid this, where the provider supports binding an XPU
endpoint to a host completion queue (see EP above), is a proxy thread on
the host that reads that queue periodically: it frees resources
independently of the kernel, which then only has to retry its calls.
.PP
Message ordering (see msg_order in \f[CR]fi_endpoint\f[R](3)) is defined
against the order in which operations are submitted.
For device\-side calls that order is defined only between calls the
application has ordered: calls made by one thread in program order, or
calls of different threads that the application orders through
synchronization of its own.
Concurrent calls of different threads of a kernel, such as calls that
threads reach at the same instruction, have no defined submission order
between them, so no message ordering applies between their operations.
.PP
A call made at a scope the provider does not support returns
\-FI_EOPNOTSUPP before submitting anything.
Any other failure is reported as a negative fabric errno, defined in
\f[CR]rdma/fi_errno.h\f[R].
.PP
A completion queue or counter call made by one scope behaves as one call
of its host counterpart does (see \f[CR]fi_cq\f[R](3) and
\f[CR]fi_cntr\f[R](3)).
At a group scope the buffer of a completion queue read is shared by the
scope, so it must be memory every thread of the scope can access.
